<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest. Apple requires one for App Store submission, and this app had none.

  Everything here is a statement about a product that collects nothing: no analytics, no network,
  no accounts, no IAP, no tracking. `PRODUCT.md` makes the absence a feature, and
  `docs/PRE-DEPLOYMENT-CHECKLIST.md` §5 requires it to be verified by inspection — this file is
  where that verification is declared to Apple rather than only to ourselves.

  The one required-reason API this app touches is file timestamps, through `FileManager` in
  `CoachWorldSaveStore`: writing the save atomically and checking whether one exists. Reason code
  C617.1 is "times for files inside the app container, to display to the person or for the app's own
  use" — which is exactly what a single-save career file is.

  If a later change adds a category — user defaults, disk space, system boot time — it gets a row
  here in the same commit. A manifest that silently falls behind the code is worse than none,
  because it is a claim rather than an omission.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array/>
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
            <array>
                <string>C617.1</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
